      "title": "Enzyme Structure & Components",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-II-i-j-17",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Define enzymes and their biological role.</li><li>Describe the components of an enzyme.</li><li>Explain the lock-and-key and induced fit models.</li><li>Identify the active site and substrate.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Enzyme</strong> — a biological catalyst, usually a protein.</li><li><strong>Substrate</strong> — the molecule an enzyme acts on.</li><li><strong>Active site</strong> — the region where the substrate binds.</li><li><strong>Cofactor</strong> — non-protein helper (e.g., metal ions).</li><li><strong>Coenzyme</strong> — organic helper (e.g., NAD⁺, FAD).</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. What Is an Enzyme?</h4><p>Enzymes are proteins that speed up chemical reactions by lowering activation energy. They are not consumed in the reaction.</p><h4>B. Components of an Enzyme</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Apoenzyme</td><td>Protein portion</td></tr><tr><td>Cofactor</td><td>Non-protein helper (metal ions: Zn²⁺, Mg²⁺)</td></tr><tr><td>Coenzyme</td><td>Organic helper (NAD⁺, FAD, CoA)</td></tr><tr><td>Holoenzyme</td><td>Apoenzyme + cofactor/coenzyme</td></tr><tr><td>Active site</td><td>Where substrate binds</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>C. Models of Enzyme Action</h4><ul><li><strong>Lock-and-key</strong> — active site fits substrate exactly.</li><li><strong>Induced fit</strong> — active site adjusts to fit substrate better.</li></ul>",
      "activity1": {
        "timeLimit": 90,
        "badgeId": "enzyme-master",
        "badgeName": "Enzyme Master",
        "badgeIcon": "🧪",
