      "title": "Oxidation & Reduction Reactions",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-II-i-j-18",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Define oxidation and reduction.</li><li>Explain oxidation-reduction (redox) reactions.</li><li>Identify oxidizing and reducing agents.</li><li>Explain the role of electron carriers.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Oxidation</strong> — loss of electrons (LEO).</li><li><strong>Reduction</strong> — gain of electrons (GER).</li><li><strong>Redox reaction</strong> — coupled oxidation & reduction.</li><li><strong>Electron carrier</strong> — molecule that shuttles electrons (NAD⁺, FAD).</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. LEO GER</h4><ul><li><strong>L</strong>ose <strong>E</strong>lectrons = <strong>O</strong>xidation</li><li><strong>G</strong>ain <strong>E</strong>lectrons = <strong>R</strong>eduction</li></ul><h4>B. Redox in Biology</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Reaction</th><th>What Happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>NAD⁺ + 2e⁻ + H⁺ → NADH</td><td>NAD⁺ is reduced (gains electrons)</td></tr><tr><td>NADH → NAD⁺ + 2e⁻ + H⁺</td><td>NADH is oxidized (loses electrons)</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>C. Electron Carriers</h4><ul><li><strong>NAD⁺/NADH</strong> — key carrier in respiration</li><li><strong>FAD/FADH₂</strong> — another carrier</li><li><strong>NADP⁺/NADPH</strong> — carrier in photosynthesis</li></ul>",
